import GroupApproximation.Higman.TransportFive
import GroupApproximation.Higman.BenignTorsionFree

/-!
# Higman's Section 5: un-conjugation, reduced to one graph subgroup

`Higman.Transport.UnConjugation` (`Higman/TransportFive.lean`) is the leaf the
transport of Section 5 cannot reach with the proved benign calculus:

    (★)   `BenignTF (Seq.ASub B)  →  BenignTF (Subgroup.closure (Seq.bElt '' B))` ,

for every set `B` of sequences --- from the family of conjugates `a^{b_f}` to
the family of conjugators `b_f`.  The companion memo
`notes/W6_TRANSPORT_FIVE_SIZING_2026-08-22.md` records four refuted routes to
it.  This file replaces the whole of `(★)` --- a statement quantified over all
`B` --- by benignness of **one** parameter-free subgroup of **one** fixed
finitely presented torsion-free group.

## Why a subgroup and not a map

The obstruction the memo records for the image route is absolute, and §1 proves
it: for *any* group `Q`, any homomorphism `ψ : F₃ →* Q` and any homomorphism
`u : ↥K →* Q` with `ψ (a^h) = u h` for every `h ∈ K`, one gets `u h = 1` for
every `h` (`eq_one_of_unconj`).  Reading `h` off `a^h` is therefore not the
restriction of a homomorphism into anything, and no `mapEmb`, `comap` or
`congr` can perform it; `not_endo_unconj` is the same statement in the memo's
own terms, that no endomorphism of `F₃` un-conjugates.  What *is* available is
the graph of the un-conjugation as a **subgroup**, and that is what this file
uses.

## The graph, and the three identities

The conjugates `a^h`, `h ∈ K`, are a free basis of the subgroup they generate
(`Conj.cbHom_injective`), so `of h ↦ h` and `of h ↦ a^h` are two homomorphisms
out of `FreeGroup ↥K` and can be paired:

    `graphHom = cbHom.prod evalHom : FreeGroup ↥K →* F₃ × F₃` ,   `of h ↦ (a^h, h)` ,
    `graphSub = graphHom.range = ⟨(a^h, h) : h ∈ K⟩ ≤ F₃ × F₃` .

Writing `coordSub T = ⟨of h : h ∈ T⟩ ≤ FreeGroup ↥K` for a set `T` of
conjugators, three identities carry the whole reduction, and all three are
proved here:

* `map_graphHom_eq` --- `(coordSub T).map graphHom = graphSub ⊓ ((coordSub T).map cbHom).prod ⊤`.
  This is the **only** place `Conj.cbHom_injective` is load-bearing, and what it
  buys is the one implication
  `cbHom u ∈ (coordSub T).map cbHom → u ∈ coordSub T`:
  an element of the graph whose first coordinate is a word in the `a^h` with
  `h ∈ T` came from a word in the `of h` with `h ∈ T`, so its second coordinate
  is a word in `T` too.  Without it the meet would be too big --- the first
  coordinate would no longer determine the word --- and the reduction would fail
  here and nowhere else.
* `sup_prod_bot_eq` --- joining with the first factor `⊤ × ⊥` clears the first
  coordinate: `(coordSub T).map graphHom ⊔ ⊤ × ⊥ = ⊤ × (coordSub T).map evalHom`.
  The memo's fourth refuted route attempts exactly this inside `F₃`, where
  `⟨a⟩ ⊔ ASub B` stays inside the normal closure of `a` and meets `K` trivially;
  in the double it is one multiplication, `(a^h, h) · ((a^h)⁻¹ x, 1) = (x, h)`.
* `comap_inr_prod_top` --- reading the second factor back is a `comap` along
  `MonoidHom.inr`.

With `BenignTF.inf`, `BenignTF.sup`, `BenignTF.comap` and `benignTF_of_fg` these
give `benignTF_map_evalHom`, and at `T = Seq.bK '' B` --- where the first map is
`Seq.ASub B` by `Seq.ASub_eq_map` (`Higman/SequenceSpace.lean`, part of the
sequence-space layer, not of the transport) and the second is
`Subgroup.closure (Seq.bElt '' B)` --- that is exactly `(★)`.

## What is left

`ConjugatorGraph`, one field: `BenignTF graphSub`.  Nothing in it mentions `B`,
sequences, recursive enumerability or the row; `graphSub_eq_closure` spells it
as the subgroup of `F₃ × F₃` generated by the pairs `(a^h, h)`, `h ∈ K`.  Its
generating set is recursively enumerable, so Higman's theorem predicts it
benign; what is missing is an explicit witness.

Five things to say about the residual, and they are the honest ones.

* It is **sufficient**, not known to be necessary: nothing here derives
  `BenignTF graphSub` back from `(★)`, so this is a reduction and not a
  reformulation.
* The stable-letter slot `benignTF_of_stable` of `Higman/RowDeletionBenign.lean`
  is stated at an abstract finitely presented torsion-free base, and `F₃ × F₃`
  is one, so it can be aimed at `graphSub` directly; the device of
  `Higman/RowSubgroup.lean` --- a bijective ascending HNN extension whose stable
  letter walks the generating family --- is the concrete pattern to re-run.  What
  a letter has to do here is send `(a^h, h)` to `(a^{hk}, hk)`.  Since `graphSub`
  is free on the pairs (`graphHom_injective`) and `h ↦ h k` permutes the index
  set `K`, that assignment *is* an automorphism of `graphSub`, so a descent has
  something to run on.  Two things it must get past, and `not_endo_unconj` is
  half of the second: `graphSub` is not finitely generated, so it cannot itself
  be the associated subgroup of a finitely presented HNN extension; and the
  obvious way to keep the associated subgroups finitely generated --- a letter
  conjugating the first factor `F₃ × 1` onto the graph of an endomorphism `ρ` of
  `F₃` --- would need `ρ (a^h) = h`, which `not_endo_unconj` refutes.  The index
  move is right multiplication, and right multiplication is not a homomorphism of
  the index group, so a witness has to find its finitely generated associated
  subgroups somewhere other than a graph of an endomorphism.
* Only the index set matters, not that it is all of `K`: the proof of
  `map_graphHom_eq` uses just that the graph is taken over a set containing `T`,
  so the graph over any set of conjugators containing `Seq.bK '' B` --- the coded
  family alone, for instance --- serves equally well, and may be the easier
  target.
* The witness must be torsion-free, since the leaf is `BenignTF`, and that costs
  nothing: every construction in `Higman/BenignTorsionFree.lean` carries the
  clause through direct products and HNN extensions.  An amalgam is no longer
  expensive either --- `Higman.AmalgamTorsion.isPowerTorsionFree_pushoutI` became
  unconditional on 2026-08-22, delegating to
  `PushoutITorsionFree.isPowerTorsionFree_pushoutI_of_nonempty`
  (`Algebra/PushoutITorsionFree.lean`), and the two inputs it used to be gated on
  were deleted rather than proved.  Do not read that as making amalgams over
  arbitrary subgroups available: it settles the *torsion* clause only, and
  `Amalgam.isFinitelyPresented_push` (`Higman/AmalgamPushout.lean:173`) still
  carries `[Group.FG G]` on the glued subgroup, which is a separate hypothesis
  and the binding one below.  Note also that `graphSub` is not finitely generated
  --- it is indexed by all of `K` --- so `benignTF_of_fg` will not close it: the
  remaining work is a construction, not bookkeeping.
* The route to try first, recorded as a proposal and **not proved here**.  Put
  `W := FreeGroup ↥K × F₃`.  The diagonal of `F₃ × F₃` is finitely generated,
  hence benign; its preimage `Δ.comap (evalHom.prodMap (MonoidHom.id F₃))` is the
  graph of `evalHom` inside `W`, and is benign *in `W`* by `BenignTF.comap` ---
  the `TorsionFreeFPOvergroup W` that step needs is `F₃ × F₃` itself, along the
  injective `cbHom.prodMap (MonoidHom.id F₃)`.  And `graphSub` is the image of
  that graph along the very same injective map into the finitely presented
  `F₃ × F₃`.  That last step is exactly `BenignTF.mapEmb` but for its
  `[Group.FG G]` hypothesis, which `W` fails.  So the construction collapses to
  one general lemma --- `mapEmb` without finite generation of the source,
  equivalently "benign in benign is benign" --- which this repository does not
  have, and which would serve the whole benign lane rather than this leaf alone.
  Whether it holds in that generality is open here, and that is why the residual
  is left as `ConjugatorGraph` and not restated as the general lemma: the witness
  `mapEmb` builds is an amalgam, and finite presentation of an amalgam wants the
  glued subgroup finitely generated.  What makes a workaround plausible in this
  instance is that the map is not an arbitrary embedding --- its image already
  sits inside the target, and `ker Seq.retract` is benign by `BenignWitness.ker`
  with a concrete direct-product witness whose `L` is itself a graph
  (`Benign.lean:247`) --- so the combined witness may be buildable by hand as a
  direct product, with nothing glued.

**Nothing inhabits `ConjugatorGraph`.**
-/

namespace GroupApproximation
namespace Higman
namespace Star

open GroupApproximation.Higman.Conj

/-! ## 1.  No homomorphism un-conjugates -/

/-- **Un-conjugation is not induced by any homomorphism.**  If a homomorphism
`ψ` out of `F₃` sends every conjugate `a^h` to `u h` for a homomorphism `u` on
the conjugating subgroup, then `u` is trivial.

The reason is one evaluation: at `h = 1` the conjugate is `a` itself and `u 1 =
1`, so `ψ a = 1`; and then every `ψ (a^h)` is a conjugate of `1`.

This retires the route permanently, and not only for this lane.  The memo's
first refuted route argues that no *endomorphism of `F₃`* induces the
un-conjugation; the statement here quantifies over every group `Q` and every
homomorphism into it, so no enlargement of `F₃`, no change of ambient group and
no later choice of coding can recover a homomorphic un-conjugation.  A subgroup
construction is therefore forced, rather than merely unavoidable by the routes
tried so far. -/
theorem eq_one_of_unconj {Q : Type} [Group Q] (psi : F₃ →* Q) (u : ↥K →* Q)
    (hu : ∀ h : ↥K, psi ((h : F₃)⁻¹ * a * (h : F₃)) = u h) (h : ↥K) : u h = 1 := by
  have hone : psi a = 1 := by
    have h1 := hu 1
    have hcoe : (((1 : ↥K) : F₃))⁻¹ * a * ((1 : ↥K) : F₃) = a := by
      show (1 : F₃)⁻¹ * a * (1 : F₃) = a
      group
    rw [hcoe, map_one] at h1
    exact h1
  have h2 := hu h
  rw [map_mul, map_mul, map_inv, hone, mul_one, inv_mul_cancel] at h2
  exact h2.symm

/-- The homomorphism counting occurrences of `b`.  Only used to see that `b` is
not trivial. -/
def bCount : F₃ →* Multiplicative ℤ :=
  FreeGroup.lift fun i : Fin 3 => if i = 1 then Multiplicative.ofAdd (1 : ℤ) else 1

theorem bCount_b : bCount b = Multiplicative.ofAdd (1 : ℤ) := by
  unfold bCount b
  rw [FreeGroup.lift_apply_of]
  simp

theorem b_ne_one : (b : F₃) ≠ 1 := by
  intro hb
  have h : bCount b = 1 := by
    rw [hb, map_one]
  rw [bCount_b] at h
  have h' : (1 : ℤ) = 0 := congrArg Multiplicative.toAdd h
  exact one_ne_zero h'

/-- **No endomorphism of `F₃` un-conjugates**, the memo's first refuted route in
its own terms.  It falls straight out of `eq_one_of_unconj` at the inclusion of
the conjugating subgroup, since `b ≠ 1`.

It is worth stating separately because it refutes the first shape a
stable-letter attack on `graphSub` would reach for.  An HNN extension of
`F₃ × F₃` is finitely presented only when its associated subgroups are finitely
generated, and the cheapest such pair is the first factor `F₃ × 1` together with
the graph `{(x, ρ x) : x ∈ F₃}` of an endomorphism `ρ` of `F₃`, the isomorphism
between them being `(x, 1) ↦ (x, ρ x)`.  A letter of that shape carries
`(a^h, 1)` to `(a^h, ρ (a^h))`, so it produces the generators `(a^h, h)` of the
graph exactly when `ρ (a^h) = h` --- which is what this theorem refutes.  A
witness therefore has to move the index by something other than the graph of an
endomorphism. -/
theorem not_endo_unconj (rho : F₃ →* F₃) :
    ¬ ∀ h : ↥K, rho ((h : F₃)⁻¹ * a * (h : F₃)) = (h : F₃) := by
  intro hrho
  have hb : (b : F₃) = 1 := eq_one_of_unconj rho K.subtype hrho ⟨b, b_mem_K⟩
  exact b_ne_one hb

/-! ## 2.  The conjugator graph -/

/-- The evaluation homomorphism: a word in the conjugators, read back in
`F₃`. -/
def evalHom : FreeGroup ↥K →* F₃ := FreeGroup.lift fun h : ↥K => (h : F₃)

theorem evalHom_of (h : ↥K) : evalHom (FreeGroup.of h) = (h : F₃) := by
  unfold evalHom
  rw [FreeGroup.lift_apply_of]

/-- The generating pairs of the graph: the conjugate `a^h` beside the
conjugator `h`. -/
def genPair (h : ↥K) : F₃ × F₃ := ((h : F₃)⁻¹ * a * (h : F₃), (h : F₃))

/-- **The graph map**, `of h ↦ (a^h, h)`.  Its first component is the free
basis map `Conj.cbHom`; that is what makes it injective. -/
def graphHom : FreeGroup ↥K →* F₃ × F₃ := cbHom.prod evalHom

theorem graphHom_apply (w : FreeGroup ↥K) : graphHom w = (cbHom w, evalHom w) := rfl

theorem graphHom_of (h : ↥K) : graphHom (FreeGroup.of h) = genPair h := by
  rw [graphHom_apply, cbHom_of, evalHom_of]

theorem graphHom_injective : Function.Injective graphHom := by
  intro x y hxy
  have h : cbHom x = cbHom y := congrArg Prod.fst hxy
  exact cbHom_injective h

/-- **The conjugator graph** `⟨(a^h, h) : h ∈ K⟩ ≤ F₃ × F₃`.  Benignness of this
one subgroup is all of Higman's Section 5 that the transport still owes. -/
def graphSub : Subgroup (F₃ × F₃) := graphHom.range

theorem mem_graphSub {x : F₃ × F₃} : x ∈ graphSub ↔ ∃ w, graphHom w = x := by
  unfold graphSub
  exact MonoidHom.mem_range

/-- **The graph, spelled by generators.** -/
theorem graphSub_eq_closure : graphSub = Subgroup.closure (Set.range genPair) := by
  refine le_antisymm ?_ ?_
  · intro x hx
    obtain ⟨w, rfl⟩ := mem_graphSub.mp hx
    refine FreeGroup.induction_on w ?_ ?_ ?_ ?_
    · rw [map_one]
      exact Subgroup.one_mem _
    · intro h
      rw [graphHom_of]
      exact Subgroup.subset_closure ⟨h, rfl⟩
    · intro h hmem
      rw [map_inv]
      exact Subgroup.inv_mem _ hmem
    · intro u v hu hv
      rw [map_mul]
      exact Subgroup.mul_mem _ hu hv
  · refine (Subgroup.closure_le _).mpr ?_
    rintro _ ⟨h, rfl⟩
    exact mem_graphSub.mpr ⟨FreeGroup.of h, graphHom_of h⟩

/-! ## 3.  The three identities -/

/-- The coordinate subgroup of the free group on the conjugators cut out by a
set of conjugators. -/
def coordSub (T : Set ↥K) : Subgroup (FreeGroup ↥K) :=
  Subgroup.closure (FreeGroup.of '' T)

/-- **The graph over a set of conjugators is cut out of the whole graph by the
first coordinate.**  This is where `Conj.cbHom_injective` is spent: the first
coordinate of a graph element determines the word it came from, so knowing that
coordinate lies in the subgroup generated by `{a^h : h ∈ T}` forces the whole
element into the graph over `T`. -/
theorem map_graphHom_eq (T : Set ↥K) :
    (coordSub T).map graphHom
      = graphSub ⊓ ((coordSub T).map cbHom).prod (⊤ : Subgroup F₃) := by
  refine le_antisymm ?_ ?_
  · rintro _ ⟨w, hw, rfl⟩
    refine Subgroup.mem_inf.mpr ⟨mem_graphSub.mpr ⟨w, rfl⟩, ?_⟩
    rw [graphHom_apply]
    exact Subgroup.mem_prod.mpr ⟨⟨w, hw, rfl⟩, Subgroup.mem_top _⟩
  · intro x hx
    obtain ⟨hx₁, hx₂⟩ := Subgroup.mem_inf.mp hx
    obtain ⟨w, rfl⟩ := mem_graphSub.mp hx₁
    rw [graphHom_apply] at hx₂
    obtain ⟨v, hv, hveq⟩ := (Subgroup.mem_prod.mp hx₂).1
    have hveq' : cbHom v = cbHom w := hveq
    have hvw : v = w := cbHom_injective hveq'
    exact Subgroup.mem_map.mpr ⟨w, hvw ▸ hv, rfl⟩

/-- **Joining with the first factor clears the first coordinate.**  Inside `F₃`
this step is impossible --- `⟨a⟩ ⊔ ASub B` stays in the normal closure of `a`,
which meets `K` trivially --- and that is the memo's fourth refuted route.  In
the double it is one multiplication: `(a^h, h) · ((a^h)⁻¹ x, 1) = (x, h)`. -/
theorem sup_prod_bot_eq (T : Set ↥K) :
    (coordSub T).map graphHom ⊔ (⊤ : Subgroup F₃).prod (⊥ : Subgroup F₃)
      = (⊤ : Subgroup F₃).prod ((coordSub T).map evalHom) := by
  refine le_antisymm (sup_le ?_ ?_) ?_
  · rintro _ ⟨w, hw, rfl⟩
    rw [graphHom_apply]
    exact Subgroup.mem_prod.mpr ⟨Subgroup.mem_top _, ⟨w, hw, rfl⟩⟩
  · intro y hy
    obtain ⟨-, hy₂⟩ := Subgroup.mem_prod.mp hy
    refine Subgroup.mem_prod.mpr ⟨Subgroup.mem_top _, ?_⟩
    rw [Subgroup.mem_bot.mp hy₂]
    exact Subgroup.one_mem _
  · intro x hx
    obtain ⟨-, hx₂⟩ := Subgroup.mem_prod.mp hx
    obtain ⟨w, hw, hweq⟩ := hx₂
    have hleft : graphHom w
        ∈ (coordSub T).map graphHom ⊔ (⊤ : Subgroup F₃).prod (⊥ : Subgroup F₃) :=
      Subgroup.mem_sup_left (Subgroup.mem_map.mpr ⟨w, hw, rfl⟩)
    have hright : (((cbHom w)⁻¹ * x.1, (1 : F₃)) : F₃ × F₃)
        ∈ (coordSub T).map graphHom ⊔ (⊤ : Subgroup F₃).prod (⊥ : Subgroup F₃) :=
      Subgroup.mem_sup_right
        (Subgroup.mem_prod.mpr ⟨Subgroup.mem_top _, Subgroup.one_mem _⟩)
    have hmul := Subgroup.mul_mem _ hleft hright
    have heq : graphHom w * (((cbHom w)⁻¹ * x.1, (1 : F₃)) : F₃ × F₃) = x := by
      refine Prod.ext ?_ ?_
      · show cbHom w * ((cbHom w)⁻¹ * x.1) = x.1
        group
      · show evalHom w * (1 : F₃) = x.2
        rw [mul_one]
        exact hweq
    rwa [heq] at hmul

/-- **Reading the second factor back.** -/
theorem comap_inr_prod_top (H : Subgroup F₃) :
    ((⊤ : Subgroup F₃).prod H).comap (MonoidHom.inr F₃ F₃) = H := by
  refine Subgroup.ext fun x => ?_
  constructor
  · intro hx
    exact (Subgroup.mem_prod.mp (Subgroup.mem_comap.mp hx)).2
  · intro hx
    exact Subgroup.mem_comap.mpr
      (Subgroup.mem_prod.mpr ⟨Subgroup.mem_top _, hx⟩)

/-- A product with the top subgroup is a preimage along the first
projection. -/
theorem prod_top_eq_comap_fst (A : Subgroup F₃) :
    A.prod (⊤ : Subgroup F₃) = A.comap (MonoidHom.fst F₃ F₃) := by
  refine Subgroup.ext fun x => ?_
  constructor
  · intro hx
    exact Subgroup.mem_comap.mpr (Subgroup.mem_prod.mp hx).1
  · intro hx
    exact Subgroup.mem_prod.mpr ⟨Subgroup.mem_comap.mp hx, Subgroup.mem_top _⟩

/-! ## 4.  The ambient plumbing -/

/-- `F₃` is its own torsion-free finitely presented overgroup. -/
def f3Overgroup : TorsionFreeFPOvergroup F₃ where
  K := F₃
  torsionFree := isPowerTorsionFree_freeGroup
  emb := MonoidHom.id _
  emb_injective := Function.injective_id

/-- The double `F₃ × F₃` is its own torsion-free finitely presented overgroup:
finitely presented by `ProductFinitePresentation.instProd`, torsion-free by
`IsPowerTorsionFree.prod`. -/
def doubleOvergroup : TorsionFreeFPOvergroup (F₃ × F₃) where
  K := F₃ × F₃
  torsionFree :=
    IsPowerTorsionFree.prod isPowerTorsionFree_freeGroup isPowerTorsionFree_freeGroup
  emb := MonoidHom.id _
  emb_injective := Function.injective_id

/-- The first factor of the double is the image of the left inclusion. -/
theorem map_inl_top :
    (⊤ : Subgroup F₃).map (MonoidHom.inl F₃ F₃)
      = (⊤ : Subgroup F₃).prod (⊥ : Subgroup F₃) := by
  refine le_antisymm ?_ ?_
  · rintro _ ⟨y, -, rfl⟩
    refine Subgroup.mem_prod.mpr ⟨Subgroup.mem_top _, ?_⟩
    show (1 : F₃) ∈ (⊥ : Subgroup F₃)
    exact Subgroup.one_mem _
  · intro x hx
    obtain ⟨-, hx₂⟩ := Subgroup.mem_prod.mp hx
    refine Subgroup.mem_map.mpr ⟨x.1, Subgroup.mem_top _, ?_⟩
    refine Prod.ext rfl ?_
    show (1 : F₃) = x.2
    exact (Subgroup.mem_bot.mp hx₂).symm

/-- The first factor of the double is finitely generated: it is the image of a
free group of rank three. -/
theorem fg_prod_bot : ((⊤ : Subgroup F₃).prod (⊥ : Subgroup F₃)).FG := by
  have htop : (⊤ : Subgroup F₃).FG := by
    refine (Subgroup.fg_iff _).mpr
      ⟨Set.range (FreeGroup.of : Fin 3 → F₃), ?_, Set.finite_range _⟩
    exact FreeGroup.closure_range_of (Fin 3)
  have hmap := fg_map htop (MonoidHom.inl F₃ F₃)
  rwa [map_inl_top] at hmap

/-! ## 5.  The transfer -/

/-- **The transfer, at an arbitrary set of conjugators.**  Given the graph, the
subgroup generated by a set of conjugators is benign as soon as the subgroup
generated by the matching conjugates is: intersect the graph with the first
coordinate, join with the first factor, read the second factor back. -/
theorem benignTF_map_evalHom (hgraph : BenignTF graphSub) (T : Set ↥K)
    (hA : BenignTF ((coordSub T).map cbHom)) :
    BenignTF ((coordSub T).map evalHom) := by
  letI : Group.FG (F₃ × F₃) :=
    ProductFinitePresentation.fg_of_isFinitelyPresented (F₃ × F₃)
  have hprod : BenignTF (((coordSub T).map cbHom).prod (⊤ : Subgroup F₃)) := by
    rw [prod_top_eq_comap_fst]
    exact BenignTF.comap doubleOvergroup (MonoidHom.fst F₃ F₃) hA
  have hgamma : BenignTF ((coordSub T).map graphHom) := by
    rw [map_graphHom_eq]
    exact BenignTF.inf hgraph hprod
  have hbot : BenignTF ((⊤ : Subgroup F₃).prod (⊥ : Subgroup F₃)) :=
    benignTF_of_fg
      (IsPowerTorsionFree.prod isPowerTorsionFree_freeGroup isPowerTorsionFree_freeGroup)
      fg_prod_bot
  have hjoin : BenignTF ((coordSub T).map graphHom
      ⊔ (⊤ : Subgroup F₃).prod (⊥ : Subgroup F₃)) := BenignTF.sup hgamma hbot
  rw [sup_prod_bot_eq] at hjoin
  have hfin := BenignTF.comap f3Overgroup (MonoidHom.inr F₃ F₃) hjoin
  rwa [comap_inr_prod_top] at hfin

/-! ## 6.  The leaf, and `(★)` -/

/-- **What Section 5 still costs: one subgroup.**  The conjugator graph
`⟨(a^h, h) : h ∈ K⟩ ≤ F₃ × F₃` is benign, with a torsion-free witness.

No set of sequences, no recursive enumerability and no row structure appears:
this is a single statement about one fixed subgroup of one fixed finitely
presented torsion-free group.  `graphSub_eq_closure` spells its generators.

**Nothing inhabits this structure.** -/
structure ConjugatorGraph where
  /-- The conjugator graph is benign, with a torsion-free witness. -/
  graph_benign : BenignTF graphSub

/-- The conjugates of a coded family are what `Seq.ASub` names. -/
theorem coordSub_bK_map_cbHom (B : Set Seq.E) :
    (coordSub (Seq.bK '' B)).map cbHom = Seq.ASub B := by
  unfold coordSub
  exact (Seq.ASub_eq_map B).symm

/-- The conjugators of a coded family are what `(★)` asks about. -/
theorem coordSub_bK_map_evalHom (B : Set Seq.E) :
    (coordSub (Seq.bK '' B)).map evalHom = Subgroup.closure (Seq.bElt '' B) := by
  unfold coordSub
  rw [MonoidHom.map_closure]
  congr 1
  refine Set.ext fun x => ?_
  constructor
  · rintro ⟨_, ⟨_, ⟨f, hf, rfl⟩, rfl⟩, rfl⟩
    exact ⟨f, hf, (evalHom_of (Seq.bK f)).symm⟩
  · rintro ⟨f, hf, rfl⟩
    exact ⟨FreeGroup.of (Seq.bK f), ⟨Seq.bK f, ⟨f, hf, rfl⟩, rfl⟩,
      evalHom_of (Seq.bK f)⟩

/-- **`(★)`, from the graph.**  Higman's Section 5 leaf `Transport.UnConjugation`
holds as soon as the conjugator graph is benign. -/
theorem unConjugation_of_graphBenign (hgraph : BenignTF graphSub) :
    Transport.UnConjugation where
  unconj := by
    intro B hB
    have hA : BenignTF ((coordSub (Seq.bK '' B)).map cbHom) := by
      rw [coordSub_bK_map_cbHom]
      exact hB
    have h := benignTF_map_evalHom hgraph (Seq.bK '' B) hA
    rwa [coordSub_bK_map_evalHom] at h

/-- **`(★)`, from the leaf.** -/
theorem unConjugation_of (hgraph : ConjugatorGraph) : Transport.UnConjugation :=
  unConjugation_of_graphBenign hgraph.graph_benign

